Centrally Mediated Abdominal Pain Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Continuous intractable abdominal pain, Unassociated with eating/defecation, Disproportionate functional disability, Normal comprehensive GI investigations

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-005
Reason for Visit: Intractable, severe abdominal pain with resultant acute functional collapse and inability to cope at home.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with continuous, highly frequent intractable abdominal pain causing profound distress and physical disability. An urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to physically examine the abdomen to definitively exclude an acute surgical abdomen (e.g., perforation, ischemia, peritonitis), safely manage the acute pain crisis, and prevent a likely unnecessary, high-stress emergency department presentation prior to daytime primary care availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach has been in constant, blinding agony all day and night, nothing touches the pain, I can't function."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with continuous, nearly constant severe abdominal pain that has spiked over the last 12 hours. The pain is distinctly unassociated with physiological events like eating, defecation, or positioning. There is a complete lack of response to typical gastrointestinal antispasmodics and simple analgesics taken today. The pain has caused profound social disability, preventing the patient from leaving bed. Patient has a known history of severe anxiety and fibromyalgia. Pain has been present for over six months but today's exacerbation is intolerable.
Functional Status: Bedbound strictly due to subjective pain severity; unable to complete ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver (child) is highly distressed by the patient's agony; high anxiety environment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/90 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Lying in fetal position, groaning, eyes closed, appearing disproportionately distressed relative to physical findings.
Physical Exam: Completely normal general abdominal examination. Abdomen is soft, non-distended, with normal active bowel sounds. Complete absence of localized peritonism, guarding, or rigidity. No palpable masses or organomegaly. Rovsing's, Murphy's, and psoas signs are completely negative. Digital rectal examination deferred as no lower GI symptoms reported. Documented history of normal comprehensive abdominal imaging and endoscopies within the last year.
Environmental Observations: Bedside table cluttered with multiple empty anti-spasmodic and analgesic packets.
Medication Reconciliation: Has exceeded maximum daily dose of Hyoscine and paracetamol with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Centrally Mediated Abdominal Pain Syndrome - Status: Severe acute exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis / Surgical Abdomen - Ruled out due to completely soft abdomen, no localized tenderness, no guarding, and normal temperature.

Biliary Colic - Ruled out as pain is continuous rather than colicky, unassociated with eating, and negative Murphy's sign.

Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out due to absence of vomiting, normal bowel sounds, and soft, non-distended abdomen.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to a severe, intractable exacerbation of continuous abdominal pain resulting in acute functional collapse. Despite a chronic history, the sudden spike in symptom severity mimics an acute surgical abdomen, necessitating an immediate hands-on clinical examination. Palpating a completely soft, non-peritonitic abdomen allowed the physician to safely exclude surgical emergencies like perforation or ischemia. Without this immediate clinical exclusion and subsequent pharmacological de-escalation, the patient's profound distress would have inevitably resulted in an ambulance transfer to the ED. The necessity of this urgent physical exam to ensure patient safety fulfills the requirements of MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg to break the acute pain cycle via non-opioid pathways. Advised strict avoidance of opioids which may exacerbate central sensitization.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict routine of 10 minutes of guided mindfulness or meditation immediately when pain begins to escalate, rather than immediately reaching for ineffective analgesics. Reducing physiological stress decreases central pain signaling, rewarding the behavioral shift with improved symptom control.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to chronic pain clinic and clinical psychology.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad for comfort.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 48 hours to discuss long-term central neuromodulators (e.g., SNRIs, TCAs).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing bilious/bloody vomiting, absolute constipation, high fever, or if the abdomen becomes rigid and hard to touch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This acute management assumes no new structural pathology has developed since recent normal imaging.
Clinical Course: This syndrome involves the brain amplifying normal gut signals into severe pain. It is a chronic condition that does not respond to traditional painkillers but can improve significantly with nerve-targeting medications and psychological strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop a high fever, start vomiting blood or green fluid, or if your stomach becomes swollen and hard as a board.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bowel structurally is completely healthy, but the 'pain thermostat' in your brain is turned up too high. Traditional painkillers don't lower the thermostat, but specific nerve medications and stress-reduction techniques will.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking extra antispasmodics as they will not work and may cause side effects. Use a heating pad to provide a soothing alternative sensory input to the brain, which can help 'distract' the nervous system from the pain signals.

